WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.965
Three hundred and fifty-nine thousand, two hundred and fifty-one registered lobbying communications across twelve industry sectors.

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:08.295
Oil and gas.

00:00:08.295 --> 00:00:09.147
Banking.

00:00:09.147 --> 00:00:10.022
Pharma.

00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:10.977
Big tech.

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:16.806
Each sector card shows communications breakdown, top organizations, and target government bodies.

00:00:16.806 --> 00:00:23.045
The pattern is universal: every sector's number one lobbying target is the government body that regulates it.

00:00:23.045 --> 00:00:25.670
Oil and gas lobby Natural Resources.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:27.477
Banks lobby Finance.

00:00:27.477 --> 00:00:29.329
Pharma lobbies Health Canada.

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:33.261
The regulator and the regulated are in constant private communication.

00:00:33.261 --> 00:00:34.704
That is not oversight.

00:00:34.704 --> 00:00:35.840
That is capture.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:44.386
Concentration index analysis — the Herfindahl measure of sector dominance — shows which industries have consolidated lobbying power into the fewest firms.

00:00:44.386 --> 00:00:48.590
Criminal Code sections are cited for relevant conflict-of-interest statutes.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:49.886
The data speaks.

00:00:49.886 --> 00:00:51.238
The law exists.

00:00:51.238 --> 00:00:52.727
Enforcement does not.
